Esterases are enzymes that hydrolyse esters.

{{figure:pdf_visual_01.png}}

Enzymes can be quite specific in the structures of the substrates they act upon. For example, an esterase isolated from the mould Aspergillus niger will hydrolyse phenyl ethanoate, $\text{CH}_3\text{CO}_2\text{C}_6\text{H}_5$, but not its isomer methyl benzoate, $\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{CO}_2\text{CH}_3$.
